<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manasseh of Judah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manasseh was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> for about half a century in the 7th century BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>. He remained a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and is named among the tributaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, and his reign saw economic recovery after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s invasion. The Bible condemns him as the worst of Judah's kings for restoring foreign cults, and blames him for the later fall of the kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manasseh-of-judah.jpg" alt="Manasseh of Judah" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manases-Manasseh.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
